---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Switches & Optics"
  description="Quantum-X800, Q3400 and the transceivers hanging off them - and the reach budget that decides where a rack is physically allowed to stand."
  heading="Switches, Optics and Cabling"
  slug="/switches-optics"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      <a href="/anatomy#spine">Chapter 2 of Part I</a> described a copper spine chosen over optics
      because transceivers would have cost <S id="optical-penalty" />. That argument does not stop
      at the rack wall - it reappears here, one tier out, with the distances multiplied by a
      hundred and the answer reversed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="generations">Two generations, and why the distinction matters</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        InfiniBand comes in generations, each roughly twice as fast as the last, and each with a
        three-letter name. The two in service here are NDR and XDR: NDR carries
        <S id="ndr-rate" /> down one port, XDR carries <S id="xdr-rate" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters more than a spec-sheet detail, because the two are often talked about as if
        they were the same thing. The racks in a shipping SuperPOD today are usually joined by the
        older, cheaper generation - not the newest one in the press release.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        A port is four lanes. NDR runs them at 100 Gb/s each for <S id="ndr-rate" /> a port; XDR
        runs them at 200 Gb/s for <S id="xdr-rate" />. The switch families are Quantum-2 and
        Quantum-X800 respectively, and NVIDIA is explicit that Quantum-2 is positioned "for
        Hopper-generation or cost-optimized deployments".<Cite ids="nv-ib-switching" />
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is exactly what a GB200 SuperPOD uses. NVIDIA's own reference architecture specifies
        QM9700 switches, with each compute tray carrying "four ConnectX-7 (CX-7) NICs to support
        InfiniBand NDR (400Gbps) connectivity".<Cite ids="nv-superpod-components" /> Quantum-X800
        and ConnectX-8 are the newer pairing, and they arrive with GB300. Both are real; they are
        different design points, not different marketing for the same one.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        The radix is where the generations diverge in a way that changes topology rather than
        throughput. A <S id="q3400-ports" bare /> Gb/s Q3400 presents 144 ports over 72 OSFP
        cages - two ports per cage - which is roughly 115 Tb/s of switching in one
        box.<Cite ids={['nv-xdr-switches', 'nv-quantum-x800']} /> There is also a
        <S id="q3200-ports" bare /> Gb/s Q3200 in a 2U air-cooled chassis, which NVIDIA positions
        for large or mixed-generation environments.
      </p>
      <p>
        Radix is the lever that decides how many tiers a fabric needs, and therefore how many hops
        and how much optics it buys. <a href="/topology">The next chapter but one</a> works that
        arithmetic; the number to carry there is 144.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="reach">Reach is the real constraint</h2>

    <p>
      A switch port does not care what is plugged into it. The floor plan does.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Link budget"
    hint="Drag the distance · pick a medium"
    caption="Reach figures are NVIDIA's for 1.6 Tb/s OSFP cabling: active copper is specified at 1.1–3 m, and the twin-port 2×DR4 transceiver at up to 500 m over 1310 nm single-mode fibre. Power and cost are indicative orders of magnitude for the class of cable, not quoted per-part figures - they are here to show the shape of the trade, not to price a build."
  >
    <div data-budget>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 220" role="img"
        aria-label="A distance scale from half a metre to 500 metres showing which cabling media can close a link at each distance, with the selected distance marked.">
        <g data-scale></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Distance</dt><dd data-out="dist">3 m</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Medium</dt><dd data-out="medium">Active copper</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Link closes</dt><dd data-out="closes">yes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Power per link</dt><dd data-out="power">~2 W</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Relative cost</dt><dd data-out="cost">1×</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="verdict" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="ctl">
        <label for="dist">Distance<output data-out="dist-lbl">3 m</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="dist" data-dist min="0" max="100" value="20" />
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Cabling medium">
        <button type="button" data-medium="passive" aria-pressed="false">Passive copper</button>
        <button type="button" data-medium="active" aria-pressed="true">Active copper</button>
        <button type="button" data-medium="mmf" aria-pressed="false">Multimode</button>
        <button type="button" data-medium="smf" aria-pressed="false">Single-mode</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        Copper at these rates reaches a few metres. NVIDIA specifies its 1.6 Tb/s active copper
        cable at <S id="xdr-copper-reach" />, which is enough to cross a rack or reach the one
        beside it and nothing further.<Cite ids="nv-xdr-switches" /> Optics reach
        <S id="xdr-optics-reach" /> over single-mode fibre - two orders of magnitude more, at a
        cost and a power draw per link that copper does not have.
      </p>
      <p>
        That gap is why a fabric's physical design is mostly an exercise in keeping links short.
        Every metre past three is a transceiver, and every transceiver is watts and dollars
        multiplied by a port count in the thousands.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Run the multiplication and the in-rack argument reappears at cluster scale. The spine
        inside one NVL72 avoided roughly <S id="optical-penalty" /> of transceiver power by staying
        copper over a metre and a half. A fabric joining 128 racks cannot make that choice: the
        distances are tens to hundreds of metres, so the optics are not optional and the power is
        simply a cost of doing business.
      </p>
      <p>
        The consequence is a hierarchy that shows up in every real floor plan. Rail links from a
        compute tray to its leaf switch are kept inside the copper envelope wherever the layout
        allows; leaf-to-spine hops go optical because they must cross rows. Which is also why
        <a href="/topology#rails">rail alignment</a> is worth so much - it converts the traffic
        that dominates a collective into the hop that is cheapest to build.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        Copper is cheap, cool and short. Optics are expensive, hot and long. Every topology
        decision in the next two chapters is, underneath, a decision about how many links land on
        the wrong side of that line.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="numbers">Switch and cabling figures</h2>

    <SpecTable
      caption="The physical plant"
      ids={['xdr-rate', 'ndr-rate', 'q3400-ports', 'q3200-ports', 'xdr-copper-reach', 'xdr-optics-reach', 'sharp-v4']}
    />

    <Deeper title="What the generation ladder looks like, and how firmly">
      <p>
        Each InfiniBand generation has doubled the per-lane rate: HDR at 50 Gb/s a lane and 200
        Gb/s a port, NDR at 100 and 400, XDR at 200 and 800. The rates for the two generations in
        service here are confirmed against NVIDIA's own switch documentation and appear as specs
        above.<Cite ids={['nv-ib-switching', 'nv-xdr-switches']} />
      </p>
      <p>
        The older rungs - SDR through EDR - are widely published but the IBTA's own roadmap
        presents them only as an image, so this site does not state them as sourced figures. That
        is a smaller point than it sounds: nothing in this section depends on the historical
        ladder, only on the two generations actually deployed. It is noted on
        <a href="/sources#gaps">the sources page</a> rather than quietly rounded over.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    interface Medium {
      id: string; name: string; maxM: number; watts: string; cost: string; blurb: string;
    }

    /*
     * Reach is NVIDIA's published figure for 1.6 Tb/s OSFP cabling. Power and
     * cost are indicative for the class - the widget caption says so - and are
     * here to show the shape of the trade rather than to price a build.
     */
    const MEDIA: Medium[] = [
      { id: 'passive', name: 'Passive copper', maxM: 1.5, watts: '~0 W', cost: '0.5×',
        blurb: 'No electronics at all - just wire. Free to run and impossible to stretch. In practice this is an intra-rack cable and nothing else.' },
      { id: 'active', name: 'Active copper', maxM: 3, watts: '~2 W', cost: '1×',
        blurb: 'Copper with signal conditioning at the ends, specified by NVIDIA at 1.1 to 3 m. This is the workhorse for a tray reaching its own rack’s leaf switch, and the last option before optics.' },
      { id: 'mmf', name: 'Multimode optics', maxM: 50, watts: '~10 W', cost: '4×',
        blurb: 'Short-reach light. Cheaper transceivers than single-mode and enough reach to cross a row, which is why leaf-to-spine hops inside a hall reach for it before single-mode.' },
      { id: 'smf', name: 'Single-mode optics', maxM: 500, watts: '~15 W', cost: '8×',
        blurb: 'NVIDIA’s twin-port 2×DR4 transceiver over 1310 nm fibre reaches 500 m - enough to span a building. The reach that makes large fabrics buildable, and the line item that makes them expensive.' },
    ];

    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-budget]');
    if (root) {
      const fig = root.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const scale = root.querySelector('[data-scale]')!;
      const slider = fig.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-dist]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };
      let medium = 'active';

      // 0.5 m to 500 m, logarithmic - the interesting decisions are all in the
      // first few metres, which a linear scale would compress to nothing.
      const D_MIN = 0.5, D_MAX = 500;
      const dist = () => D_MIN * Math.pow(D_MAX / D_MIN, Number(slider.value) / 100);
      const x = (m: number) => 40 + (Math.log10(m / D_MIN) / Math.log10(D_MAX / D_MIN)) * 640;
      const fmt = (m: number) => m < 10 ? m.toFixed(1) + ' m' : Math.round(m) + ' m';

      const render = () => {
        // Compare the value the reader is shown, not the raw one. A slider
        // position yielding 3.013 m displays as "3.0 m", and telling someone a
        // 3.0 m link will not close on a cable rated to 3 m is just confusing.
        const d = Number(dist().toFixed(1));
        const med = MEDIA.find((m) => m.id === medium)!;
        const closes = d <= med.maxM;

        out('dist').textContent = fmt(d);
        out('dist-lbl').textContent = fmt(d);
        out('medium').textContent = med.name;
        out('closes').textContent = closes ? 'yes' : 'no';
        out('closes').style.color = closes ? 'var(--ink)' : 'var(--danger)';
        out('power').textContent = med.watts;
        out('cost').textContent = med.cost;

        const cheapest = MEDIA.find((m) => d <= m.maxM);
        out('verdict').textContent = closes
          ? `${med.name} closes a ${fmt(d)} link. ${med.blurb}`
          : cheapest
            ? `${med.name} stops at ${fmt(med.maxM)}, so a ${fmt(d)} link needs ${cheapest.name.toLowerCase()} - ${cheapest.watts} and ${cheapest.cost} the cost, per link, multiplied by every port in the fabric.`
            : `Nothing in this list reaches ${fmt(d)}. Past 500 m the question stops being which cable and starts being whether these racks belong in the same fabric at all.`;

        // ── Draw the reach scale ──────────────────────────────────────
        scale.textContent = '';
        scale.appendChild(el('line', { x1: 40, y1: 172, x2: 680, y2: 172, class: 'axis' }));
        for (const tick of [0.5, 1, 3, 10, 50, 100, 500]) {
          scale.appendChild(el('line', { x1: x(tick), y1: 172, x2: x(tick), y2: 178, class: 'axis' }));
          const t = el('text', { x: x(tick), y: 192, 'text-anchor': 'middle' });
          t.textContent = tick < 1 ? '0.5 m' : tick + ' m';
          scale.appendChild(t);
        }

        MEDIA.forEach((m, i) => {
          const y = 26 + i * 32;
          const on = m.id === medium;
          const bar = el('rect', { x: x(D_MIN), y, width: x(m.maxM) - x(D_MIN), height: 18, rx: 3,
            fill: on ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--line-strong)', opacity: on ? 0.9 : 0.45 });
          scale.appendChild(bar);
          const label = el('text', { x: x(m.maxM) + 8, y: y + 13,
            fill: on ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--ink-3)' });
          label.textContent = `${m.name} - ${fmt(m.maxM)}`;
          scale.appendChild(label);
        });

        // The selected distance, drawn through every bar.
        scale.appendChild(el('line', { x1: x(d), y1: 18, x2: x(d), y2: 172,
          stroke: closes ? 'var(--gpu)' : 'var(--danger)', 'stroke-width': 2 }));
        const marker = el('text', { x: x(d), y: 12, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
          fill: closes ? 'var(--gpu)' : 'var(--danger)' });
        marker.textContent = fmt(d);
        scale.appendChild(marker);
      };

      slider.addEventListener('input', render);
      fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-medium]').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          medium = b.dataset.medium!;
          fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-medium]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          render();
        });
      });
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
